# The vendor tree is hashed byte-for-byte by the fingerprint gate in
# scripts/build.js. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which would
# check these files out with CRLF locally while the repo (and Vercel's Linux
# builder) hold LF — two different hashes for the same commit, and a deploy that
# fails only on Vercel. Pin the bytes so every checkout agrees.
assets/vendor/** -text

# The two glossary generators have the same problem from the other direction,
# and it bit locally rather than on deploy. scripts/glossary.js and
# scripts/glossary-backlinks.js each rebuild a marked region, write it with LF,
# and in --check mode compare the result to what is on disk BYTE FOR BYTE. With
# autocrlf converting these files to CRLF on checkout, that comparison comes
# back different on every Windows machine and identical on the Linux builder —
# so `npm run check` refused to publish over a difference that is not in the
# commit at all, and the suggested fix ("run npm run glossary") could not help,
# because rewriting the file changes bytes git normalises straight back.
#
# Pinning is the fix rather than teaching each checker to ignore carriage
# returns: a generator that claims a file is byte-for-byte reproducible should
# be checked byte for byte, and the thing that was actually wrong is that one
# commit had two different working copies.
glossary.html -text
labs/*.html -text

# The game pages sit under the same byte-for-byte contract and never got the
# pin. The freshness gate runs scripts/games.js --check, which regenerates
# every games/*.html in memory and compares it to the committed copy with a
# plain string equality — the glossary case again, so the same failure is
# waiting: a CRLF working copy against the generator's LF output is a
# difference that exists in no commit, fails on only one kind of machine, and
# cannot be fixed by regenerating, because git normalises the rewritten bytes
# straight back.
#
# partials/*.html is pinned for the same comparison one step removed:
# games.js STAMPS the partials' bytes into every page it generates (and the
# chrome gate reads them as the canonical chrome besides), so a CRLF checkout
# of the partials changes the generated bytes and fails the freshness gate on
# pages nobody edited. Pin both so every checkout agrees.
games/*.html -text
partials/*.html -text
